---
Part XIV: Mermaid diagrams
Chapter 1: Declaring the Mermaid kind
Trope: Mermaid diagrams
---

# Declaring the Mermaid kind

A diagram often says in one picture what prose takes a page to say: the order of a pipeline, the states of a game, the parts of a system and how they meet. Mermaid is a language for such diagrams, written as plain text, so a diagram lives in a loom as easily as code does. The Mermaid trope lets a book write a diagram in a box of the `Mermaid` kind, whose body is the diagram's Mermaid source.

```
::: The passes {Mermaid}
flowchart LR
  parse --> product --> tangle
:::
```

The box's name is its caption, as for any box. Without the trope, Loom reads `Mermaid` as a kind nobody gives a box, so the box is a fault. Declaring the kind is what makes it none. Contract 1 lets a trope declare kinds and nothing more, so the editor and the woven page show a `Mermaid` box as the source it holds.

This part is the trope's own, as a trope published for other books keeps to a part of its own. This chapter is the trope's root, since it names the trope with its `Trope:` line, and it holds the trope's manifest, as Part XIII, Tropes, describes. It lists no entries, so it is the trope's only entry.

## What the trope declares {Manifest}

The trope declares one kind of box. Its `body` names the format the box holds, `mermaid`, for whatever later draws the diagram from it.

=>

{
  "contract": "1",
  "contributes": {
    "kinds": { "box": [{ "kind": "Mermaid", "body": "mermaid" }] }
  }
}
